00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
There are intuitive questions that anyone would ask to him/herself when first hearing about the bitcoin currency

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.520
Such: what does bitcoin look like? What texture does it exactly have?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.440
Is it similar to the dollar texture, riyal or dirham! we know those currencies,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.440
we can hold and keep them in a wallet. But what does bitcoin look like?

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.040
I'm Ashraf Ibrahim, and this is our fourth episode of our new series on digital currencies "Crypto Economic Informant"

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.480
Bitcoin is a digital currency that cannot be printed in a traditional currency printing press,

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.600
nor touched or placed in a bank vault or in our wallet as we normally do with common currencies

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.520
Therefore, they are called virtual currencies, and technically Bitcoin It is a series of

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.800
a binary number system used by computers and consists of two symbols: "0" (zero) and "1" (one). .

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.320
In order to keep a balance of bitcoins or any other currencies, there must be a wallet.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.320
This wallet will have a private key, a public key and an address.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.720
They are random long codes of numbers and letters linked through

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.160
an encryption algorithm that was used to program Bitcoin at the time of its release.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.560
So, what is the public Key, the private key and the wallet address?

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.720
As for the private key, it is a secret encryption code and only you know it.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.800
Through it you can control the digital currency / bitcoin balances that you have in your wallet.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.400
Exactly like the PIN code on your bank card that you use anywhere.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:48.640
If someone knows your private key, they can easily withdraw all your bitcoin balance.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.280
It looks like that one shown on the screen. This is an example of a private key that is supposed

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:03.600
to be very secret, and if you forget or lose it, you will lose your bitcoins or digital currencies

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.880
You will not be able to make any transaction, whether selling or sending bitcoins to anyone;

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.160
with this private key you can make a digital signature on the transaction in order to complete it.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.120
Considering the public key, it looks like that one shown on the screen,

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.280
it is long and more complicated to issue. So, what happens now!

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.840
An address gets derived from it. In short, it is minimized to a smaller address.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.880
Thereby, we now have the address that we have derived from the Public Key. It is like the one shown on the screen.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.120
The address thereby is the heading by which other people can send you bitcoins or any other digital currencies

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.720
Consider it exactly like your bank account number (IBAN).

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.240
For someone to transfer money to your bank account, they need an account number.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.720
You can give it to all people no worries. Here as well, your address is the destination whereby anyone can

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.920
send you encrypted currencies, and it will be visible to everyone in the blockchain.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.800
Thus, if you decide to get into the world of digital currencies,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:11.040
you must create a wallet through which the private key and the public key are created and the address is derived.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.040
The two (private and public keys) are mathematically related to each other.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.120
The public key is mathematically derived from the private key.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:27.560
However, it is impossible to figure out a private key out of a public key due to the one way hash function.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.040
How to make private and public keys and addresses?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.400
As aforementioned, there must be a wallet in order to be able to send and receive cryptocurrencies.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.160
In order to make the wallet, One must first create the private and public key and the address.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:47.440
This can be done through decentralized platforms such as blockchain.com or myetherwallet.com

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.160
First we make the private key. Through it the public key is made,

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:58.480
yet, if one loses or forgets the private key, then all their encrypted currencies are considered lost forever, as said.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.800
So, what happens if you lose your public key? No problem, you can retrieve it again via the private key.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:09.600
A simple example: I have 5 bitcoins with me, I want to send 2 bitcoins to a friend,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.720
I will ask him/her to send their public key so I can send them the 2 bitcoins.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.040
When he/she sends me the public key, which is considered his/her address that people can transfer to

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.200
, I would log into my wallet and send the 2 bitcoins. I must sign this transaction with my private key,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.360
which proves that I am the owner of those two bitcoins.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.000
Has it been sent now? Well.. who makes sure that the transaction is right?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.400
And I, for example, do not slander this friend?

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.360
I may have copied the code for these currencies and sold it to more than one person,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.440
who makes sure that things are going well? For example, if I have 10 dollars,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.640
as soon as I give them to someone or pay them for any purpose.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.840
They are gone out of my hand because there is no other copy of them.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:58.640
But the situation in cryptocurrencies is different, because they are codes that can be copied easily like emails, for example,

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.320
as if you exactly send a copied email with the exact same text to many people.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.320
So who can verify the authenticity of the Bitcoin transferred to anyone?

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.120
Those who watched our past episodes will easily know the answer.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.680
Those who confirm are the miners (those who mine Bitcoin),

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.920
they perform a complicated process through their devices in order to verify

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.080
the transaction that took place between me and my friend, along with a few other transactions.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:29.280
When the verify the transaction it gets recorded in a block, then included in the blockchain.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:35.520
It is clear inside the block that the public key which code is (in this scenario it is my public key) sent 2 bitcoins for the second public key which is (my friend's public key).

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:44.000
Just as illustrated in front of you; What appears in the blockchain is Public keys only without names or any other details.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:54.840
After adding this block to the blockchain, I cannot either cancel or withdraw the 2 bitcoins back.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.680
Their digital ownership of them went to the other party (which is my friend).

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.360
Only, he/she can exchange them through their private key.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.120
They are now free to sell them or to pay them in exchange for anything or even send them to anyone else on their address.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.000
This was a brief review covering the most important digital currency "Bitcoin"

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.040
- in addition to public and private keys of digital currencies in general.

